---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
const bodies = [
  {name:"Asteroids",where:"Mostly the main asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, but also near-Earth space and other orbital groups.",detail:"Rocky or metallic remnants and fragments. Their sizes range from tiny stones to bodies hundreds of kilometres across. They are not all located in one belt."},
  {name:"Comets",where:"Often originate in distant reservoirs; their orbits can bring them into the inner Solar System.",detail:"Icy bodies that can develop a coma and tails when sunlight warms their surfaces. The tails are shaped by sunlight and the solar wind, and generally point away from the Sun."},
  {name:"Meteoroids",where:"Small natural objects travelling through interplanetary space.",detail:"The IAU uses a rough working size range of about 30 micrometres to 1 metre for a meteoroid. When a solid object enters an atmosphere, the light and associated heat, shock and ionisation are called a meteor; surviving natural material that reaches the ground is a meteorite."},
  {name:"Trojan asteroids",where:"Near stable regions associated with a planet's orbit, especially Jupiter's leading and trailing Lagrange points.",detail:"These objects share a planet's orbit while occupying gravitationally stable regions roughly 60 degrees ahead of or behind the planet."},
  {name:"Kuiper Belt",where:"Beyond Neptune, in the distant outer Solar System.",detail:"A broad population of icy bodies, including Pluto, and the source region for many short-period comets. It contains a range of dynamical populations."},
  {name:"Scattered disc",where:"A distant region beyond Neptune, linked to gravitational scattering by the giant planets.",detail:"Objects often have eccentric and inclined orbits, with dynamical histories shaped by Neptune and other gravitational interactions."},
  {name:"Detached objects",where:"At large distances beyond Neptune.",detail:"Their orbits are less strongly coupled to Neptune's present-day scattering than typical scattered-disc objects. Sedna is a famous example of a detached extreme object."},
  {name:"Oort Cloud",where:"A hypothesised, roughly spherical reservoir at enormous distances from the Sun.",detail:"It has not been directly observed as a cloud. Its existence is inferred from the orbital distribution of long-period comets and models of Solar System evolution."}
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Asteroids, comets and the outer frontier" description="Explore asteroids, comets, meteoroids, Trojans, the Kuiper Belt, scattered disc, detached objects and the hypothesised Oort Cloud.">
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/explore/">Explore</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Small bodies & frontier</span></div>
  <header class="page-intro container"><div class="eyebrow">ASTEROIDS, COMETS AND SMALL BODIES</div><h1>Asteroids, comets<br />and other small bodies.</h1><p class="lede">Asteroids, comets and distant icy objects preserve material left over from the Solar System's formation. Their surfaces and orbits also record later collisions and gravitational changes.</p></header>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="region-banner"><div class="region-sun"></div><div class="region-orbit"></div><div class="region-copy"><span class="eyebrow">ORBITAL GROUPS</span><h2>Small-body orbital groups</h2><p>Small bodies occupy different orbital regions and dynamical populations. Their paths can be shaped by planets, resonances, collisions and the Sun's gravity.</p><span class="region-caption">Conceptual illustration · Not to scale</span></div></div>
    <div class="body-grid">{bodies.map((body,i)=><article class="card body-card"><span class="body-index">{String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</span><h3>{body.name}</h3><span class="body-where">{body.where}</span><p>{body.detail}</p></article>)}</div>

    <section class="asteroid-reference">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">ASTEROIDS / ROCKY AND METALLIC REMNANTS</span><h2>The main asteroid belt</h2></div><p>Most asteroids orbit the Sun in the main belt between Mars and Jupiter. The region is vast and sparse: spacecraft can pass through without dodging rocks.</p></div>
      <div class="asteroid-image-grid">
        <figure><img src="https://d2pn8kiwq2w21t.cloudfront.net/images/jpegPIA22485.width-1024.jpg" alt="NASA Dawn spacecraft close view of the bright deposits in Ceres's Occator Crater." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Ceres · NASA Dawn · Bright salt deposits in Occator Crater</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia15/pia15678/PIA15678.jpg?w=1200" alt="Global view of asteroid Vesta made from images acquired by NASA's Dawn spacecraft." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Vesta · NASA Dawn · Global image mosaic</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><img src="https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/bennu-banner-1920x640-1.jpg?w=1536" alt="NASA OSIRIS-REx image of asteroid Bennu." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Bennu · NASA OSIRIS-REx · Asteroid surface</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="asteroid-size-card">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">LARGEST MAIN-BELT BODIES</span><h3>The largest main-belt objects</h3><p>Ceres is the largest body in the belt and is classified as a dwarf planet. High-resolution shape modelling gives volume-equivalent diameters of about 939.4 km for Ceres, 523.2 km for Vesta, 511 km for Pallas and 433 km for Hygiea.</p></div>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Ceres</b><span>939.4 ± 0.2 km · dwarf planet</span></li>
          <li><b>Vesta</b><span>523.2 ± 0.2 km · differentiated rocky body</span></li>
          <li><b>Pallas</b><span>511 ± 4 km · high-inclination orbit</span></li>
          <li><b>Hygiea</b><span>433 ± 8 km · carbon-rich body</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="asteroid-note">These are volume-equivalent diameters, not single equatorial measurements. Values and reported uncertainties follow the VLT/SPHERE large-asteroid shape survey by Vernazza et al. (2021). Different methods and shape models can yield different size estimates. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/202141781" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the peer-reviewed study ↗</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="asteroid-type-grid">
        <article><span class="type-chip c-type">C</span><h3>Carbon-rich</h3><p>Dark, primitive material that can include carbon-bearing compounds, hydrated minerals, clay-like silicates and organic matter.</p></article>
        <article><span class="type-chip s-type">S</span><h3>Silicate-rich</h3><p>Mostly rocky silicate minerals, commonly with metal-bearing phases such as iron-nickel.</p></article>
        <article><span class="type-chip m-type">M</span><h3>Metal-rich</h3><p>Some are rich in iron and nickel. Psyche is a complex metal-rich target, but it should not be described as a solid ball of pure metal.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="asteroid-note">These are broad spectral/compositional classes, not perfect ingredient labels. Real asteroids are diverse, can be mixtures, and may have been altered by heating, water, impacts or space weathering.</p>
      <div class="named-asteroids">
        <h3>Notable asteroids and missions</h3>
        <p><strong>Vesta</strong> is differentiated, with a basaltic crust, mantle and iron-rich core. <strong>Bennu</strong> is a carbon-rich rubble-pile near-Earth asteroid; OSIRIS-REx returned 121.6 g of samples in 2023. <strong>Ryugu</strong> is another carbon-rich rubble pile sampled by Hayabusa2. <strong>Itokawa</strong> was visited by Hayabusa. <strong>Psyche</strong> is a metal-rich asteroid being studied by NASA's Psyche mission. <strong>Didymos and Dimorphos</strong> became the target of the DART kinetic-impact test.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="comet-reference">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">COMETS / ICY BODIES</span><h2>What happens when a comet nears the Sun?</h2></div><p>Comets contain volatile ices, dust, rocky material and carbon-bearing compounds. They preserve material from cold regions of the early Solar System.</p></div>
      <div class="comet-image-grid">
        <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia17/pia17485/PIA17485.jpg?w=1536&h=804&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="Enhanced image of the dark potato-shaped nucleus of Comet Halley taken by ESA's Giotto spacecraft." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>1P/Halley · ESA Giotto · Enhanced image of the nucleus, about 15 km long</figcaption></figure>
        <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia18/pia18423/PIA18423.jpg?w=726&h=523&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="Rosetta spacecraft view of Comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko · ESA Rosetta</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="comet-terms">
        <div><b>Nucleus</b><span>The solid icy, dusty and rocky body.</span></div>
        <div><b>Coma</b><span>The temporary cloud of gas and dust around an active nucleus.</span></div>
        <div><b>Dust tail</b><span>Dust pushed and curved by sunlight pressure.</span></div>
        <div><b>Ion tail</b><span>Ionized gas guided by the solar wind and interplanetary magnetic field.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout"><strong>A large nucleus is still not a planet.</strong><p>Hubble's analysis of comet C/2014 UN271 (Bernardinelli–Bernstein) estimated a nucleus roughly 80–85 miles (about 129–137 km) across. It was too distant to resolve directly as a surface image; the size was inferred by modelling and subtracting the surrounding coma, then combining observations.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="meteor-reference">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">METEOROIDS / METEORS / METEORITES</span><h2>Meteoroid, meteor and meteorite.</h2></div><p>Small fragments can come from asteroids, comets, the Moon and even Mars. Their name depends on where they are and what happens to them.</p></div>
      <div class="meteor-stage-row">
        <div><span>01</span><h3>Meteoroid</h3><p>A natural solid object travelling through interplanetary space. The IAU uses a rough working size range of about 30 micrometres to 1 metre; the limits are conventions, not sharp physical boundaries.</p></div>
        <div><span>02</span><h3>Meteor</h3><p>The light and associated physical phenomena — including heat, shock and ionisation — produced when a solid object enters a planet's atmosphere at high speed.</p></div>
        <div><span>03</span><h3>Meteorite</h3><p>A piece that survives passage through the atmosphere and reaches the ground.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="meteorite-types">
        <h3>What meteorites are made of</h3>
        <p><strong>Stony meteorites</strong> are mainly silicate minerals; chondrites contain small once-molten droplets called chondrules, while achondrites come from melted and differentiated parent bodies. <strong>Iron meteorites</strong> are mainly iron-nickel metal. <strong>Stony-irons</strong> contain substantial proportions of both silicate rock and metal.</p>
        <p>Meteor showers happen when Earth crosses a stream of dust and fragments left along a comet's or, in some cases, an asteroid's orbit. The Perseids come from comet Swift–Tuttle; the Orionids and Eta Aquariids are linked to Halley; the Geminids are associated with asteroid-like object 3200 Phaethon.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="prose body-prose">
      <h2>How to tell the terms apart</h2>
      <p><strong>Meteoroid:</strong> a natural solid object travelling through interplanetary space; the IAU working range is roughly 30 micrometres to 1 metre, with no sharp physical cutoff. <strong>Meteor:</strong> the light and associated physical phenomena — including heat, shock and ionisation — produced by a high-speed entry into a gaseous atmosphere. <strong>Meteorite:</strong> natural solid material that survives the meteor phase without being completely vaporised.</p>
      <h2>What are comet tails?</h2>
      <p>As a comet approaches the Sun, volatile ices near its surface can turn directly into gas. Escaping gas carries dust away, creating a coma and often more than one tail. The ion tail is shaped strongly by the solar wind and magnetic field; the dust tail is influenced by sunlight pressure and the comet's orbital motion. A comet's tails generally point away from the Sun, not necessarily behind its direction of travel.</p>
      <h2>The Kuiper Belt is not the Oort Cloud</h2>
      <p>The Kuiper Belt is a broad, flattened population of icy objects beyond Neptune. The Oort Cloud is a hypothesised distant, roughly spherical reservoir proposed to explain the source of many long-period comets. They are different structures, at very different scales and with different observational status.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Scientific status matters.</strong><p>The Oort Cloud has not been directly imaged or observed as a bounded cloud. It is inferred from comet orbits and dynamical models. ORRERYA labels it as hypothesised, not as a confirmed photographically mapped region.</p></div>
      <h2>Where does the heliosphere fit?</h2>
      <p>The heliosphere is the vast bubble-like region shaped by the solar wind as it interacts with interstellar space. It is not a belt of asteroids or a gravitational wall. The Sun's gravity can bind distant objects well beyond the heliosphere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="source-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">Further reading</span><h3>FURTHER READING</h3><p>Mission data and population descriptions can change as new objects are discovered.</p></div><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/asteroids-comets-meteors/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Asteroids, Comets & Meteors ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/kuiper-belt/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Kuiper Belt ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Oort Cloud ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/heliosphere/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Heliosphere ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.iau.org/static/science/scientific_bodies/commissions/f1/meteordefinitions_approved.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IAU Commission F1 — Definitions of terms in meteor astronomy ↗</a></li></ul></div>
  </section>
  <style>.region-banner{min-height:310px;position:relative;display:flex;align-items:center;padding:32px 42px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden;background:radial-gradient(ellipse at 85% 50%,rgba(42,75,117,.34),transparent 48%),#0a1220}.region-sun{position:absolute;width:85px;height:85px;left:72%;top:46%;border-radius:50%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);background:radial-gradient(circle at 30% 28%,#fff1b8,#e5a451 60%,#8c482e);box-shadow:0 0 40px #e8a85288}.region-orbit{position:absolute;width:460px;height:240px;left:72%;top:46%;border:1px solid rgba(196,213,239,.2);border-radius:50%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%) rotate(-24deg);box-shadow:0 0 0 45px rgba(196,213,239,.025),0 0 0 110px rgba(196,213,239,.025)}.region-copy{max-width:470px;position:relative;z-index:1}.region-copy h2{font-size:2.7rem;margin:10px 0}.region-copy p{max-width:390px;font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.7}.region-caption{display:block;color:var(--quiet);font-size:.68rem;margin-top:22px}.body-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:13px;margin-top:20px}.body-card{padding:19px;position:relative}.body-index{font-size:.68rem;color:var(--gold)}.body-card h3{margin:14px 0 8px;font:600 1.15rem "Space Grotesk",sans-serif}.body-where{display:block;color:var(--cyan);font-size:.7rem;line-height:1.5}.body-card p{font-size:.79rem;line-height:1.7;margin:12px 0 0}.body-prose{margin-top:55px}.source-panel{margin-top:40px}@media(max-width:900px){.body-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.region-sun{left:86%}.region-orbit{left:86%}}@media(max-width:550px){.body-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.region-banner{padding:25px 20px;min-height:340px}.region-sun{left:100%;opacity:.55}.region-orbit{left:100%;opacity:.5}.region-copy h2{font-size:2.2rem}}</style>

  <style>
    .asteroid-reference,.comet-reference,.meteor-reference{margin:58px 0}
    .asteroid-image-grid,.comet-image-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:12px;margin:20px 0}
    .asteroid-image-grid figure,.comet-image-grid figure{margin:0;border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden;background:#091725}
    .asteroid-image-grid img,.comet-image-grid img{width:100%;height:235px;object-fit:cover;display:block}
    .asteroid-image-grid figcaption,.comet-image-grid figcaption{padding:9px 12px;color:#cad4dd;font-size:.68rem}
    .asteroid-size-card{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr .9fr;gap:22px;padding:24px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;background:#f5f7f8}
    .asteroid-size-card h3,.named-asteroids h3,.meteorite-types h3{font:500 1.55rem Georgia,serif;color:#17354d;margin:7px 0}
    .asteroid-size-card p,.named-asteroids p,.meteorite-types p{font-size:.84rem;line-height:1.8;color:#566e82}
    .asteroid-size-card ul{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:7px}
    .asteroid-size-card li{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;padding:9px 11px;border:1px solid #e0e7ec;border-radius:6px;background:#fff}
    .asteroid-size-card li b{color:#173b55}.asteroid-size-card li span{font-size:.76rem;color:#60768a;text-align:right}
    .asteroid-type-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin-top:14px}
    .asteroid-type-grid article{padding:20px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:#fff}
    .asteroid-type-grid h3{color:#173b55;font:500 1.2rem Georgia,serif;margin:9px 0}.asteroid-type-grid p{font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.7;color:#5a7083}
    .type-chip{display:inline-grid;place-items:center;width:30px;height:30px;border-radius:50%;font-weight:800}.c-type{background:#d9d8cf;color:#4e4f48}.s-type{background:#d8c2a7;color:#684b30}.m-type{background:#d6dfe4;color:#304a5b}
    .asteroid-note{font-size:.73rem;color:#62788b;line-height:1.7;margin:12px 0}.named-asteroids{margin-top:22px;padding:20px 23px;background:#edf2f5;border-radius:8px}
    .comet-terms{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:9px;margin:15px 0}
    .comet-terms>div{padding:15px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#f4f7f8;border-radius:7px}.comet-terms b,.comet-terms span{display:block}.comet-terms b{color:#173b55}.comet-terms span{color:#5b7184;font-size:.76rem;line-height:1.55;margin-top:5px}
    .meteor-stage-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px}
    .meteor-stage-row>div{padding:21px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#f5f7f8}.meteor-stage-row span{color:#98713e;font-weight:700;font-size:.7rem}.meteor-stage-row h3{color:#173b55;font:500 1.25rem Georgia,serif}.meteor-stage-row p{font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.7;color:#596f82}
    .meteorite-types{margin-top:17px;padding:22px;background:#edf2f5;border-radius:8px}
    @media(max-width:800px){.asteroid-size-card{grid-template-columns:1fr}.asteroid-type-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.comet-terms{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}
    @media(max-width:560px){.asteroid-image-grid,.comet-image-grid,.meteor-stage-row{grid-template-columns:1fr}.asteroid-image-grid img,.comet-image-grid img{height:210px}.comet-terms{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
  </style>
</SiteLayout>
